1. HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) — Port 80
What it is: The basic language used to transfer web pages over the internet.
How it works: When you type [http://example.com](http://example.com) into a browser, your computer asks
the web server over Port 80 to send back the HTML, images, and text that make up the webpage.
Catch: It transmits data in plain text. Anyone eavesdropping on your Wi-Fi network can read what you send 
(like passwords or credit cards).

2. HTTPS (HTTP Secure) — Port 443
What it is: The encrypted, secure version of HTTP.
How it works: It uses encryption (via TLS/SSL) on Port 443 so that all data sent between your browser and
the website is scrambled.
Real-world analogy: HTTP is like sending a postcard where the mail carrier can read your message. HTTPS is
like locking your message inside an unbreakable safe before mailing it.

3. FTP (File Transfer Protocol) — Port 21
What it is: A dedicated method for uploading and downloading files between a client and a server.
How it works: Port 21 is used to establish the connection and send commands (like "list files" or "download image.jpg").
When it's used: Web developers historically used FTP to upload website files to web hosting servers.

4. SSH (Secure Shell) — Port 22
What it is: A secure, encrypted way to remotely log into and control another computer or server via a text terminal.
How it works: Admins connect over Port 22 to type command-line commands on a remote server thousands of miles away as if
they were sitting right in front of it.
Why it matters: It completely replaced older, unencrypted tools (like Telnet) because everything sent over SSH is fully encrypted.

5. DNS (Domain Name System) — Port 53
What it is: The "phonebook" of the Internet.
How it works: Computers don't understand names like google.com; they only understand IP addresses like 142.250.190.46. When you type 
a web address, your device queries a DNS server on Port 53 to translate the human-friendly name into the machine-friendly IP address.
